  "id": "Compound::DB00741 - Disease::DOID:9074",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the treatment of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) through its interaction with the corticosteroid receptor. This relationship is supported by multiple pathways, all of which share a common ancestor at Hydrocortisone and Betamethasone, both of which are corticosteroids. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone may exert a therapeutic effect on SLE by modulating the corticosteroid receptor, potentially influencing inflammatory pathways and immune responses. The presence of a direct treatment effect on SLE, coupled with the shared biological context of corticosteroids, supports the mechanistic link. However, the exact molecular mechanism remains unclear, and further research is needed to elucidate the precise pathways through which Hydrocortisone exerts its effects on SLE. The evidence is consistent with a role in immune modulation, but the extent of its therapeutic potential and the specific mechanisms involved require additional investigation.",
